  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1020 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSunday night through Friday:\n\nSynopsis: Much colder Canadian air will pour into southern Wisconsin \nbehind the departed cold front Sunday night into Monday. The front \nwill be followed by a building area of Canadian high pressure, which \nwill expand in coverage and build overhead during the Monday-Tuesday \ntime frame. Mean upper troughing will move across the central CONUS \nTuesday night through Wednesday night, though lingering high \npressure will keep much of the western Great Lakes (including \nsouthern Wisconsin) dry. With high pressure starting to nudge \neastward, global guidance hints at some potential for a return to \nsoutherly surface flow by the end of next week. Should southerly \nreturn flow be realized, some temperature moderation toward normal \nwould be possible by Friday/the conclusion of the long term period. \n\nMonday through Tuesday night: With Canadian high pressure building \nin & moving overhead, expecting the coldest stretch of the entire \nforecast period during this time frame. With either light/variable \nor northwesterly surface flow preventing milder air from leaking \nnorth, could see daytime highs struggle to get out of the 30s each \nday. Ideal conditions for radiational cooling during the overnight \nhours will lead to chilly lows, with areas away from the immediate \nLake Michigan shoreline easily dropping into the low 20s. NBM \nprobabilities for lows in the 10s continue to climb upward during \neach overnight period, particularly across the northwest half of the \nCWA. Have thus maintained deterministic NBM low temp forecasts, \nwhich bring lows in the 10s to the west of I-39 each night. Be sure \nto grab the winter jacket before heading out early next week. \n\nThanksgiving: Surface pressure forecasts from the LREF point toward \nlingering high pressure influences across the western Great Lakes, \nwhich would translate to a cool but dry holiday in southern \nWisconsin. Current forecasts from the NBM show highs in the low 40s \nwith overnight lows in the 20s. Will continue to refine the forecast \nas this portion of the period draws closer.  \n\nQuigley",
